</p>
<ul>
<li><code>GBrew.fr</code> posted news about <a href="https://www.gbrew.fr/billets/billet-la-fpgb-mini-le-meilleur-fpga-de-funnyplaying-par-defaut/">yet another FPGA-based Game Boy clone</a> called the <em>FPGB Mini</em>. I&rsquo;ll stick with my <a href="/post/2023/04/my-analogue-pocket-is-one-year-old-now/">Analogue Pocket</a> for now.</li>
<li>Another cool map: <a href="https://pinballmap.com/map?by_city_no_state=Beringen&amp;amp;address=Beringen">pinballmap.com</a> that lists venues housing pinball machines. Thanks for mentioning it, <a href="https://virtualmoose.org/">Michael</a>.</li>
<li>Victoria Lacroix <a href="https://vtrlx.ca/w/2026-08-18-i-hate-crafting.html">hates crafting in video games</a>. I think it&rsquo;s fine as long as indeed it doesn&rsquo;t become a huge brainless timesink, and more importantly, if its heavy subsystems stay mostly optional.</li>
<li>According to Hamuko, <a href="https://burakku.com/blog/on-70-dollar-games/">spending $70 on a video game is OK</a>. They defend the statement with some quick financial maths. Remember when N64 games cost $65? Add inflation and you&rsquo;re well above $70. I&rsquo;m fine either way: most indie games I think are severely <em>underpriced</em>.</li>
<li>Robert Yang wrote an interesting design review of <a href="https://www.blog.radiator.debacle.us/2023/06/design-review-of-against-storm-by.html">Against The Storm</a>, reminding me that I should pick it up again.</li>
<li>There&rsquo;s someone who spent years creating a <a href="https://github.com/SiENcE/uwpatch">texture-correct Ultima Underworld patch</a>, fixing the weird affine texture-maps you also see in early 3D PS1 games.</li>
<li>Jamie Zawinski <a href="https://www.jwz.org/blog/2026/10/explain-it-to-me-like-im-80/">explains AI to us like we&rsquo;re 80</a>. You&rsquo;ll need to understand it, because&hellip;</li>
<li>The cat is out of the bag: <a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linus-Torvalds-Claude-Code">Linus Torvalds committed Claude-assisted code into the Linux Kernel</a>. I&rsquo;ve seen people creating new forks of BSD-based OSes as a result. It&rsquo;s getting very bad.</li>
<li>Very bad indeed: the <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/gaming-industry/steam-week-in-review-steam-next-fest-has-over-8-000-demos-as-worthless-vibe-coded-slop-levels-up/">Steam Next Fest submissions are drowning in AI slop</a>.</li>
<li>Or how about a certain <a href="https://www.404media.co/proton-to-re-interview-staff-about-ai-to-see-who-gets-to-keep-their-job/">Proton CEO making bold claims</a> regarding the use of genAI: my way or the highway. The latter, please.</li>
<li>Emacs turned 50! Stefan Monnier and Michael Sperber wrote a long <a href="https://pdf.stafforini.com/monnier-2020-evolution-of-emacs.pdf">paper on the history of the editor</a> (from 2020).</li>
<li>Steve Losh&rsquo;s educational posts on Emacs continue to amaze me. Here&rsquo;s an overview of what <a href="https://stevelosh.com/blog/2016/06/symbolic-computation/">symbolic computation is</a> to help you grasp the concept of a REPL and how Emacs/ELisp handles it.</li>
<li>Natalie Weizenbaum, the granddaughter of the famous MIT professor, read her granddad&rsquo;s work <a href="https://nex-3.com/blog/computer-power-and-human-reason/">Computer Power and Human Reason</a>, which in this genAI age is especially relevant.</li>
<li>Cameron Swingle is goofing around as usual with their <a href="https://thingle.online/posts/what-game-i-think-each-villain-from-scooby-doo-and-the-cyber-chase-would-play-on-their-psp">What Game I Think Each Villain From Scooby Doo And The Cyber Chase Would Play On Their PSP</a> article.</li>
<li>I&rsquo;m a bit late to the party but in case you also missed it, here&rsquo;s <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bSdg5sgt6hY">Digital Foundry&rsquo;s Diablo II: Resurrected tech analysis</a> (on YouTube).</li>
<li>Creopard.de is a fascinating new website full of old stuff such as their <a href="https://www.creopard.de/big-box-pc-games-sammeln-faszination-an-alten-schachteln.htm">love for collecting Big Box PC Games</a>.</li>
<li>This lovely <a href="https://mystjourney.com/myst/walkthrough/">Myst Journey walkthrough website</a> might come in handy next year when we play the game as part of the DOS Game Club.</li>
<li>Speaking of old PC gaming stuff: 3Dfx Interactive is thirty years old now. A <a href="https://www.resetera.com/threads/3dfx-voodoo-graphics-accelerator-cards-were-released-30-years-ago-october-1996-pc-3d-gaming-got-good.1657213/">ResetERA thread pointed it out</a>. I might expand this into a blog post of its own someday soon.</li>
<li>Speaking of ResetERA forum posts, here&rsquo;s a cool <a href="https://www.resetera.com/threads/blobbers-1st-person-dungeon-crawlers-dungeon-rpgs-classic-rpgs.804717/">overview of &ldquo;blobber&rdquo; RPG games</a> or classic first-person dungeon crawlers.</li>
</ul>
